================================================================================
THE LOCATION DOOR AND ITS LOCK — RAW EVIDENCE
zl1 (LeEco Pro3, MSM8996pro), Ubuntu Touch 24.04-2.x, 2026-09-23
OFFLINE ONLY: every line below comes from the rootfs image mounted read-only at
/mnt/utrootfs plus upstream source at the matching version. No device was
touched (it was in EDL for this whole session).
================================================================================


0. WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/82 established that the two sides of the GPS question are (a) the UT
location stack and (b) the Android HAL, and left one line to read on the device
(`does_satellite_based_positioning`). This file settles a question that comes
*before* that one and can be settled without the device:

    WHO calls u_hardware_gps_start(), and what would have to happen for that
    call to be made for the first time on this port?

The answer has two halves, one per section below:
  §2  the door is a CLIENT REQUEST that arrives over D-Bus as
      StartPositionUpdates on a session — proven from the installed binaries.
  §3+§4 the lock in front of that door is TrustStorePermissionManager, and the
      image's own two bypass levers are both unreachable on this port because
      the v63 boot image installs a getprop stub over /usr/bin/getprop.
  §3f the first gate of that lock is disassembled in the *installed* library,
      where it turns out to be a true short circuit: it returns granted before
      `credentials.profile` is read, so the AppArmor gate and the trust-store
      agent are unreachable on that path.


