2. THE DOOR: HOW u_hardware_gps_start() IS REACHED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2a. The service really does carry the hardware provider.
    The systemd unit (/usr/lib/systemd/system/lomiri-location-service.service)
    declares

        ExecStart=/usr/bin/lomiri-location-serviced --bus system \
                  --provider gps::Provider --provider remote::Provider

    and the drop-in .../lomiri-location-service.service.d/lxc-android-config.conf
    replaces it with the wrapper

        ExecStart=/usr/libexec/lxc-android-config/lomiri-location-serviced-wrapper

    whose (getprop-dependent) else-branch is byte-for-byte the same command line:

        exec lomiri-location-serviced --bus system --provider gps::Provider \
                                      --provider remote::Provider

    The unit is ENABLED: /etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/
    lomiri-location-service.service -> /usr/lib/systemd/system/lomiri-location-service.service

2b. The hardware code lives inside liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0, not in
    providerd. providerd is the *remote* provider daemon and has neither a
    NEEDED entry for libubuntu_platform_hardware_api nor any u_hardware_* string:

      $ readelf -d usr/bin/lomiri-location-service-providerd | grep NEEDED
        libdbus-cpp.so.5, liblomiri-location-service.so.3,
        libboost_program_options.so.1.83.0, libprocess-cpp.so.3, libstdc++, libc
      $ strings usr/bin/lomiri-location-service-providerd | grep -c u_hardware
        0

    The only two objects in the whole image that reference the platform API are
    the library itself and the API library:

      $ grep -rl libubuntu_platform_hardware_api $R/usr/lib $R/usr/bin
        usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0
        usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libubuntu_platform_hardware_api.so.4.0.0

2c. The thirteen u_hardware_gps_* entry points are ordinary UND dynamic symbols
    of that library (so the call is a normal PLT call, not a dlsym):

      $ readelf -sW --dyn-syms liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep u_hardware
        5: ... UND u_hardware_gps_agps_notify_connection_not_available
       17: ... UND u_hardware_gps_start
       19: ... UND u_hardware_gps_agps_notify_connection_is_open
       58: ... UND u_hardware_gps_delete_aiding_data
       94: ... UND u_hardware_gps_new
      106: ... UND u_hardware_gps_agps_set_server_for_type
      111: ... UND u_hardware_gps_inject_xtra_data
      168: ... UND u_hardware_gps_stop
      237: ... UND u_hardware_gps_set_position_mode
      254: ... UND u_hardware_gps_agps_notify_connection_is_closed
      259: ... UND u_hardware_gps_inject_time
      262: ... UND u_hardware_gps_inject_location
      370: ... UND u_hardware_gps_delete

2d. The two call sites. Reconstructing the PLT mapping: .plt is at 0x33510, the
    resolver occupies the first 32 bytes, stub i is at 0x33530 + 16*i, and stub
    i loads GOT[0x16f048 + 8*i]. That gives

        u_hardware_gps_start   reloc index 14 -> stub 0x33610, GOT 0x16f0b8
        u_hardware_gps_new     reloc index 84 -> stub 0x33a70, GOT 0x16f2e8

    and verifying the stubs:

      $ llvm-objdump-14 -d liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | sed -n '/^   33610:/,/br/p'
        33610: adrp x16, 0x16f000
        33614: ldr  x17, [x16, #184]      ; 0x16f000 + 184 = 0x16f0b8  ✓
        33618: add  x16, x16, #184
        3361c: br   x17

    Both call sites are inside the Android hardware abstraction layer:

      $ llvm-objdump-14 -d liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep -n 0x33610
        dab84:  b   0x33610        <-- tail call, inside start_positioning()
      $ llvm-objdump-14 -d liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep -n 0x33a70
        daa64:  bl  0x33a70        <-- inside Impl::register_callbacks()

    and readelf -sW places them:

      0xdaa40 size 0x40  com::lomiri::location::providers::gps::android::
                        HardwareAbstractionLayer::Impl::register_callbacks()
      0xdaa80 size 0x170 com::lomiri::location::providers::gps::android::
                        HardwareAbstractionLayer::start_positioning()

    So u_hardware_gps_new() (create the device) and u_hardware_gps_start()
    (start it) BOTH happen inside start_positioning(), and nowhere else: the
    address 0xdaa80 appears exactly once in .text as a call target, and once in
    .data.rel.ro (file offset 0x15e580, inside the HardwareAbstractionLayer
    vtable: the neighbouring slots are 0xda910 set_assistance_mode and
    0xda9c0 set_position_mode). start_positioning() is therefore a VIRTUAL
    method, dispatched through that vtable.

  $ python3 -c "d=open('/tmp/lls.so','rb').read(); import struct; \
                print([hex(i) for i in ...])"   # see §6 for the exact one-liner
  hits for 0xdaa80: 0x47e0 (metadata), 0x29da0 (metadata), 0x15e580 (.data.rel.ro)

2e. The full chain, with every address in the installed library except the two
    ends:

      client                      D-Bus method call StartPositionUpdates
        |
        v
      com.lomiri.location.Service.Session   interface com.lomiri.location.Service.Session
        |  service::session::Skeleton::on_start_position_updates
        v
      0x8f0e4  service::session::Implementation::start_position_updates()   (384 B)
        |  engine / provider selection
        v
      0xe7f10  providers::gps::Provider::start_position_updates()            (40 B)
        |  virtual call through the HAL vtable (.data.rel.ro 0x15e580)
        v
      0xdaa80  HardwareAbstractionLayer::start_positioning()                (368 B)
        |  -> 0xdaa40  Impl::register_callbacks()   -> bl 0x33a70 = u_hardware_gps_new
        |  -> (tail)                                -> b  0x33610 = u_hardware_gps_start
        v
      libubuntu_platform_hardware_api.so.4 -> android_dlopen/android_dlsym
        -> the Android GNSS HAL (HIDL android.hardware.gnss@1.0) in the container

    Every name in that chain is present in the library's symbol table, which is
    how the middle steps were identified:

      $ readelf -sW liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep -E 'start_position_updatesEv' | c++filt
        com::lomiri::location::service::session::Implementation::start_position_updates()
        com::lomiri::location::providers::gps::Provider::start_position_updates()
        com::lomiri::location::providers::gpsd::Provider::start_position_updates()
        com::lomiri::location::providers::dummy::Provider::start_position_updates()
        ... (remote::Provider::Stub/Skeleton, FusionProvider, ProxyProvider, Engine::Controller)

    Note the two consequences, which are the whole point of this section:
      * Nothing in the daemon's *startup* touches the hardware. Constructing the
        provider, registering the D-Bus name and opening the bus do not call
        u_hardware_gps_new or u_hardware_gps_start.
      * The first position request a client ever makes is what calls them. On a
        device where no client has ever completed CreateSessionForCriteria +
        StartPositionUpdates, the hardware half has provably never been asked to
        do anything — which is a *structural* explanation of docs 82's
        observation, not a hypothesis about the HAL.


3. THE LOCK: TrustStorePermissionManager
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3a. The comparison happens before any session object exists. In
    src/location_service/com/lomiri/location/service/skeleton.cpp
    (sha256 82b52d46be05a526bdbd1bc71c2d22bd5f636bae47f3188e065a68a794efd0a6),
    handle_create_session_for_criteria():

        Criteria criteria;
        in->reader() >> criteria;

        auto credentials =
            configuration.credentials_resolver->resolve_credentials_for_incoming_message(in);

        auto result =
            configuration.permission_manager->check_permission_for_credentials(criteria, credentials);

        if (PermissionManager::Result::rejected == result) throw std::runtime_error
        {
            "Client lacks permissions to access the service with the given criteria"
        };

    and the throw lands in the catch-all that sends the client

        com.lomiri.location.Service.Error.CreatingSession / "Error creating session"

    while logging the real reason server-side. Both strings are in the installed
    binary:

      $ strings -t x liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep -E 'Client lacks|Could not resolve'
        fcbb0 Client lacks permissions to access the service with the given criteria
        fca08 Could not resolve PID

3b. The manager in use is the trust-store one, not the "no trust store" build.
    src/.../service/system_configuration.cpp (sha256
    1aeff856f922650d24222ffc97600075920e7f9a45c08520d02c1e60802830ec) chooses
    at compile time:

        clls::SystemConfiguration& clls::SystemConfiguration::instance()
        {
        #ifdef ENABLE_TRUST_STORE
            static UbuntuSystemConfiguration config;   // -> TrustStorePermissionManager
        #else
            static DefaultSystemConfiguration config;  // -> DefaultPermissionManager
        #endif
            return config;
        }

    and the Debian build sets it explicitly — debian/rules of this same package:

        -DENABLE_TRUST_STORE=ON \

    in both the 32- and 64-bit invocations.

3c. Its check, src/.../service/trust_store_permission_manager.cpp
    (sha256 4aac9da4a5010a1b602a38601581f5bde7b2afc7f5d6f2811e5020b9ea22b66f):

        service::PermissionManager::Result service::TrustStorePermissionManager::check_permission_for_credentials(
                const location::Criteria&,
                const service::Credentials& credentials)
        {
            if (is_running_under_testing())
                return Result::granted;

            if (credentials.profile.empty()) {
                SYSLOG(ERROR) << "Could not resolve PID " << credentials.pid << " to apparmor profile";
                return service::PermissionManager::Result::rejected;
            }

            std::string description = i18n::tr("wants to access your current location.");

            core::trust::Agent::RequestParameters params
            {
                core::trust::Uid{credentials.uid},
                core::trust::Pid{credentials.pid},
                credentials.profile,
                TrustStorePermissionManager::default_feature(),
                description
            };

            Result result{Result::rejected};

            try
            {
                auto answer = agent->authenticate_request_with_parameters(params);
                switch(answer)
                {
                case core::trust::Request::Answer::granted:
                    result = Result::granted;
                    break;
                case core::trust::Request::Answer::denied:
                    result = Result::rejected;
                    break;
                }
            } catch(...)
            {
                // We silently drop all issues here and return rejected.
                result = Result::rejected;
            }

            return result;
        }

    with

        bool is_running_under_testing()
        {
            return core::posix::this_process::env::get(
                        "TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING",
                        "0") == "1";
        }

    The env var name is in the installed binary:

      $ strings -t x liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION
        101e48 TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING
        101e80 ./src/location_service/com/lomiri/location/service/trust_store_permission_manager.cpp

    Three gates, in order, and every one of them ends in `rejected`:
      1. the environment bypass (off unless the env var is "1"),
      2. a non-empty AppArmor profile, resolved with aa_gettaskcon(pid) by
         DBusDaemonCredentialsResolver::libapparmor_profile_resolver() in
         skeleton.cpp — and if aa_gettaskcon fails the resolver logs
         "Could not resolve PID <pid> to apparmor profile: <e>" and leaves the
         string EMPTY, which is exactly the rejection above,
      3. the trust-store agent, whose every exception is swallowed into
         `rejected` with no client-visible reason.

3d. Where the profile comes from, same file:

        auto rc = aa_gettaskcon(pid, &scope.con, &scope.mode);
        if (rc == app_armor_error) throw std::system_error{errno, std::system_category()};
        return std::string{scope.con ? scope.con : ""};

    So the gate is "the kernel must be able to name the calling task's
    confinement label". A caller the kernel reports as `unconfined` passes this
    gate and lands on gate 3; a caller aa_gettaskcon cannot answer for does not.

3e. The trust-store side is wired up in the image, which is what makes gate 3 a
    real dependency rather than a no-op:

      /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/com.lomiri.location.Service.conf
          <allow own="core.trust.dbus.Agent.LomiriLocationService"/>
      /usr/lib/systemd/user/lomiri-location-service-trust-stored.service
          ExecStart=/usr/bin/trust-stored-skeleton --remote-agent DBusRemoteAgent \
                    --bus=system --local-agent MirAgent \
                    --trusted-mir-socket=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/mir_socket_trusted \
                    --for-service LomiriLocationService --store-bus session
          ConditionPathExists=/run/user/%U/mir_socket_trusted
      /etc/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/lomiri-location-service-trust-stored.service
          (and the -wayland variant, both enabled; the ConditionPathExists picks one)
      /etc/systemd/system/multi-user.target.wants/lomiri-location-service.service
          (the service itself, enabled)

    The matching client-side helper is right there in the library:
      $ readelf -sW liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep -i truststore | c++filt
        0xd5e10  TrustStorePermissionManager::default_feature()
        0xd5f30  TrustStorePermissionManager::create_default_instance_with_bus(...)
        0xd6110  TrustStorePermissionManager::check_permission_for_credentials(...)   (892 B)

3f. 3c is quoted from the ubports branch on gitlab. The *installed* library is
    where the gates actually run, so the function was disassembled as well --
    what follows is read out of the binary (sha256
    0095442f32820d77ec96dbc45f17c8e51f417b8a012c604fd284684adc730173, the same
    file whose strings appear above):

      $ llvm-objdump-14 -d --start-address=0xd6110 --stop-address=0xd648c \
            /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0

    First the two rodata operands of the env getter -- the two adrp/add pairs
    decode to exactly the variable name and its default, and the bytes at those
    addresses were dumped rather than assumed:

        d615c: adrp x1, 0x101000 ; d6160: add x1, x1, #3656  -> 0x101e48
        d6174: adrp x1, 0xfc000  ; d6178: add x1, x1, #976   -> 0xfc3d0
        $ (python3 dump of the file)
          0x101e48 b'TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING'
          0x101e80 b'./src/location_service/com/lomiri/location/service/trust_store_permission_manager.cpp'
          0xfc3d0  b'0'
          0xfca08  b'Could not resolve PID '

    Its result arrives as a data pointer and a length (d6190 `ldp x0, x21,
    [sp, #168]` -> x0 = data, x21 = length; d6328 `ldrb w1, [x0]` reads the
    data pointer). The comparison against the literal "1" is inlined into
    length-then-first-character:

        d6198: cmp  x21, #1        ; length != 1 ...
        d619c: b.eq 0xd6328
        d61a0: mov  w21, #0        ; ... -> under_testing = false
        d6328: ldrb w1, [x0]
        d632c: cmp  w1, #49        ; '1'
        d6334: b.ne 0xd61a0        ; not "1" -> under_testing = false
        d635c: b.eq 0xd6370        ; == "1" -> jump over everything
        d6370: mov  w19, #0
        d628c: ...                 ; d62ac: mov w0, w19 ; ret

    and the flag is re-tested on the path that falls through the string
    destructors:

        d61f0: cbnz w21, 0xd6370   ; under_testing -> return immediately
        d61f4: ldr  x0, [x19, #16] ; ONLY NOW is the profile touched
        d61fc: cbz  x0, 0xd62c8    ; length 0 -> log, and rejected

    Polarity, derived from three independent sites rather than assumed:
      * the empty-profile path (d62c8-d6324) logs "Could not resolve PID " with
        the pid and sets w19 = 1; 3c says that path returns `rejected`, so
        **w19 = 1 is rejected and w19 = 0 is granted** (i.e. `Result` has
        granted = 0);
      * the agent call is `cmp w0, #1 ; cset w19, ne` (d626c-d6270) and the
        agent answer enum, from trust-store 2.0.2 include/core/trust/request.h
        (ubports/24.04-2.x), is

            enum class Answer : std::int32_t { denied, granted };

        so denied = 0, granted = 1, and w19 = 0 exactly when the agent granted
        it -- which is the switch in 3c;
      * therefore `d6370: mov w19, #0` on the testing path is a `granted`
        return.

    Two structural facts fall out, and both matter for the experiment:

      * **The bypass is at the very top.** d61f4 reads `credentials.profile`
        (`ldr x0, [x19, #16]` is the string's length, and 3c's first test is
        `.empty()`), and it is only reachable when the under-testing flag is
        false. It is also where `default_feature()` (d6240) and the agent's
        virtual call (d6258-d6268, vtable slot `[x2, #16]`) live. So on the
        testing path `aa_gettaskcon` is never called and the agent is never
        asked: gates 2 and 3 cannot veto a caller that has already passed gate
        1, and no AppArmor profile has to be arranged for the experiment.
      * **The environment reaches the daemon.** Both branches of the wrapper
        (4a) end in `exec lomiri-location-serviced ...`, so the unit's
        environment *is* the daemon's environment; there is no `lxc-attach`,
        no `lxc-execute` and no `env -i` in between to scrub it. An
        `Environment=` drop-in on `lomiri-location-service.service` is
        sufficient, and the wrapper's own `export` (which its dead `getprop`
        gate can never reach) is not needed.

    What this still does not prove: that the unit's `ExecStart` really is this
    wrapper on the device (the probe prints it), and that nothing later in the
    session path rejects the client for a different reason.


4. THE TWO DEAD LEVERS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4a. The wrapper the unit actually runs is
    /usr/libexec/lxc-android-config/lomiri-location-serviced-wrapper:

        #!/bin/sh
        if [ -x /usr/bin/getprop ] && [ "$(getprop custom.location.testing)" = "true" ]; then
            export TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING="1"
        fi

        if [ -x /usr/bin/getprop ] && [ "$(getprop custom.location.fake)" = "true" ]; then
            lat="$(getprop custom.location.lat 51.505660)"
            lon="$(getprop custom.location.lon -0.099850)"
            exec lomiri-location-serviced --bus system \
                 --provider dummy::Provider \
                 --dummy::Provider::ReferenceLocationLat="${lat}" \
                 --dummy::Provider::ReferenceLocationLon="${lon}"
        else
            exec lomiri-location-serviced --bus system \
                 --provider gps::Provider --provider remote::Provider
        fi

    i.e. the image ships BOTH levers docs 82 recommends: the testing bypass for
    gate 1, and a whole-stack-without-Android dummy provider for the A/B.

4b. Both levers read `getprop`, and on this port `getprop` is not the binary
    that is on the image. boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init
    installs a /bin/sh stub over /usr/bin/getprop and /usr/bin/setprop on every
    boot (the block is inside `if [ -d /var/lib/lxc/android/pre-start.d ]`, which
    exists on this port; it also does `mount -o remount,rw /` first, which is how
    it can write to the image at all). The stub's whole decision table:

        prop="${1:-}"
        default="${2:-}"
        case "$prop" in
            '') exit 0 ;;
            ro.build.version.sdk) printf '%s\n' 28 ;;
            ro.product.device|ro.product.vendor.device|ro.product.odm.device) printf '%s\n' le_zl1 ;;
            ro.product.name|ro.product.vendor.name|ro.product.odm.name) printf '%s\n' ZL1_CN ;;
            ro.product.model|ro.product.vendor.model) printf '%s\n' 'LeEco Pro3' ;;
            ro.hardware|ro.boot.hardware) printf '%s\n' qcom ;;
            ro.treble.enabled) printf '%s\n' true ;;
            ro.vndk.version) printf '%s\n' 28 ;;
            sys.boot_completed|dev.bootcomplete|service.bootanim.exit)
                [ -n "$default" ] && printf '%s\n' "$default" ;;
            init.svc.*|vendor.*|persist.*|ctl.*|debug.*|test.*)
                [ -n "$default" ] && printf '%s\n' "$default" ;;
            *) [ -n "$default" ] && printf '%s\n' "$default" ;;
        esac
        exit 0

    `custom.location.testing` and `custom.location.fake` fall to `*)`. The
    wrapper calls both with NO second argument, so with $default empty the stub
    prints NOTHING and exits 0. Neither comparison can ever be true. And
    `setprop` is a documented no-op in the same script:

        if [ "$TOOL_NAME" = setprop ]; then
            log "no-attach diagnostic no-op setprop: $*"
            exit 0
        fi

    so the properties cannot be set either. The originals are parked next to the
    stubs as /usr/bin/getprop.orig-zl1 and /usr/bin/setprop.orig-zl1 (and the
    libhybris-based original needs the TLS-slot shim to run at all).

    VERDICT: on this port the wrapper's `else` branch is what always runs, and
    `TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING` is always off.
    Both of docs 82's levers are unreachable *as shipped* — not because the
    property is wrong, but because the tool the wrapper asks cannot answer.


5. THE TWO FIXES THIS LICENSES (drop-ins, both reversible, neither applied)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5a. Turn on the testing bypass without touching getprop. The service reads the
    variable from its own environment, and systemd can set it:

      /etc/systemd/system/lomiri-location-service.service.d/zl1-testing.conf
          [Service]
          Environment=TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION_MANAGER_IS_RUNNING_UNDER_TESTING=1

    /etc/systemd/system is one of the writable whitelist paths on this port, and
    the drop-in directory must be the FULL unit name (a bare
    lomiri-location-service.d/ is silently inert). Effect: gate 1 returns
    `granted` for every caller, so any process — including an unconfined root
    shell — can create a session and start position updates, which is what calls
    u_hardware_gps_new/u_hardware_gps_start for the first time.

    This is the image's own testing switch. It is still a permission bypass: for
    as long as the drop-in is in place, anything on the device can obtain the
    device's location. That is a trade the user has to make, exactly like
    `orientationsensor=False` and the debug keeper.

5b. Replace the ExecStart instead of the property, for the fake-location A/B:

      /etc/systemd/system/lomiri-location-service.service.d/zl1-dummy.conf
          [Service]
          ExecStart=
          ExecStart=/usr/bin/lomiri-location-serviced --bus system \
                    --provider dummy::Provider \
                    --dummy::Provider::ReferenceLocationLat=51.505660 \
                    --dummy::Provider::ReferenceLocationLon=-0.099850

    (the empty ExecStart= resets the list; without it systemd appends a second
    command). This is the same command the wrapper would have run had the
    property been readable, so it exercises the UT stack and not Android — the
    A/B docs 82 wanted, restored without a boot-image change.

    Gate 1 is still in front of it: with the dummy provider, CreateSessionForCriteria
    still runs the same permission check, so 5a is needed for 5b to be observable
    by a client. That, incidentally, is a second, independent reason the
    "fake location" experiment could never have produced a result on this port.


6. REPRODUCTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  R=/mnt/utrootfs            # mount rootfs-...-zl1-host.img read-only

  # the door
  readelf -sW  $R/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 | grep u_hardware
  cp $R/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/liblomiri-location-service.so.3.0.0 /tmp/lls.so
  llvm-objdump-14 -d /tmp/lls.so > /tmp/lls.asm          # objdump cannot decode aarch64
  readelf -rW /tmp/lls.so | awk '$3=="R_AARCH64_JUMP_SLOT"{n++; print n-1, $5}' | grep u_hardware_gps
  readelf -sW /tmp/lls.so | grep -E 'start_positioningEv|register_callbacksEv' | c++filt
  python3 - <<'PY'
  import struct
  d=open('/tmp/lls.so','rb').read()
  pat=struct.pack('<Q',0xdaa80); i=0; hits=[]
  while True:
      j=d.find(pat,i)
      if j<0: break
      hits.append(hex(j)); i=j+1
  print('hits for 0xdaa80:', hits)   # ['0x47e0', '0x29da0', '0x15e580']
  PY

  # the lock
  curl -s https://gitlab.com/ubports/development/core/lomiri-location-service/-/raw/ubports/24.04-2.x/debian/rules | grep ENABLE_TRUST_STORE
  curl -s .../src/location_service/com/lomiri/location/service/trust_store_permission_manager.cpp
  curl -s .../src/location_service/com/lomiri/location/service/skeleton.cpp
  strings -t x /tmp/lls.so | grep -E 'TRUST_STORE_PERMISSION|Client lacks permissions|Could not resolve PID'

  # the levers
  sed -n '1040,1110p' boot/v63/scripts/init-bottom/zl1-postswitch-debug-init
  cat $R/usr/libexec/lxc-android-config/lomiri-location-serviced-wrapper

  # the gates, in the installed binary rather than in the source
  llvm-objdump-14 -d --start-address=0xd6110 --stop-address=0xd648c /tmp/lls.so
  python3 - <<'PY'          # 0x101e48 -> the env name, 0xfc3d0 -> "0"; .rodata is vaddr==offset
  d=open('/tmp/lls.so','rb').read()
  for v in (0x101e48, 0x101e80, 0xfc3d0, 0xfca08):
      print(hex(v), d[v:v+90].split(b'\0')[0])
  PY
  curl -s https://gitlab.com/ubports/development/core/trust-store/-/raw/ubports/24.04-2.x/include/core/trust/request.h | grep -A5 'enum class Answer'


7. WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * It does not prove the hardware works once asked. It proves which call has to
    happen for the question to become answerable, and that nothing on this port
    has made it.
  * It does not prove that a session request *is* what the current device state
    lacks — only that it is the door, and that the lock in front of it is closed
    by default for any client the trust store has no answer for. The
    discriminating experiment is §5a + a real client (§ the doc's instrument).
  * It says nothing new about the Android side: whether `u_hardware_gps_start`
    returns success, whether the QMI client opens, whether a fix is ever
    obtained. docs 82's line (`locClientOpen failed`) is still the one to read.
  * It does not establish that `aa_gettaskcon` succeeds or fails for a root
    shell on this device -- but 3f narrows what that is worth: on the testing
    path it is never called at all (the bypass returns before the profile is
    read), so it decides the outcome only for a client that reaches the gate
    with the bypass still off.
  * Nothing here was measured on the device. The device was in EDL throughout.
